.TH CONFINE 1 2026-10-28 "agentutils 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
confine \- run a command that may write only to a fork of a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B confine
.RB [ \-w
.IR path ]...
.RB [ \-r
.IR path ]...
.RB [ \-\-no\-ignore ]
.RB [ \-\-rm | \-d
.IR dir ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B confine
forks the working directory, as
.BR fsfork (1)
does, next to it, named after it and the first number not taken
.RB ( turso.1
for
.BR turso ),
and runs
.I command
in the fork, or your shell
.RB ( $SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
if it is not set) if no command is given, confined: the command may write
only to the fork, and may read in your home directory only what it needs,
and so no secrets.
Given a directory with
.BR \-d ,
it runs the command in that directory, confined to it, and makes no fork.
The processes the command starts are confined the same way, and none can
undo it.
.PP
.B confine
says where the fork is on standard error before it runs the command, and
leaves it, for
.BR fsdiff (1)
to show what the command changed in it, unless the command could not be
run, or
.B \-\-rm
is given.
.PP
.B confine
does not stay: it becomes the command, which so exits as it would, with its
own status, or by the signal that ended it.
With
.BR \-\-rm ,
it waits for the command, ignoring the terminal's interrupt, quit and hangup
signals, which reach the command too, removes the fork, and then exits as
the command did.
.PP
Everything from
.I command
on belongs to the command, its own options included, so
.B confine claude \-\-resume
passes
.B \-\-resume
to
.BR claude .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " dir" "\fR, \fP\-\-dir " dir
Run the command in
.I dir
itself, and let it write there, rather than in a fork of the working
directory.
.TP
.B \-\-rm
Remove the fork, and its base, when the command exits, as if it had never
been made.
It cannot be given with
.BR \-d ,
as there is no fork to remove.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ignore
Fork what
.I .gitignore
files ignore too, which
.BR fsfork (1)
otherwise leaves out, so that builds in the fork start warm.
It cannot be given with
.BR \-d ,
as there is no fork.
.TP
.BI \-r " path" "\fR, \fP\-\-read " path
Let the command read
.I path
too, and everything under it, even in the home directory.
Can be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-w " path" "\fR, \fP\-\-write " path
Let the command read and write to
.I path
too, and to everything under it.
Can be repeated.
Commands that keep state in the home directory need it: for example,
.B confine \-w ~/.claude claude
lets Claude Code save its sessions and settings.
.SH WHAT THE COMMAND MAY DO
Everything but:
.TP
Writing
Only the fork, or
.IR dir ,
the temporary directories
.RB ( /tmp
and
.BR $TMPDIR ),
devices, the caches that builds and package managers write to
.RB ( ~/.cargo ,
.BR ~/.rustup ,
.BR ~/.cache ,
.B ~/.npm
and
.BR ~/Library/Caches ),
and the paths given with
.B \-w
may be written to.
.TP
Reading
Nothing in the home directory can be read but what may be written to, the
paths given with
.BR \-r ,
and what commands need to run: git's configuration
.RB ( ~/.gitconfig
and
.BR ~/.config/git ),
Claude Code's
.RB ( ~/.claude.json ),
toolchains and the commands installed with them
.RB ( ~/.local/bin ,
.BR ~/.local/lib ,
.BR ~/.local/share/claude ,
.BR ~/.nvm ,
.BR ~/.volta ,
.BR ~/.bun ,
.BR ~/.deno ,
.B ~/.pyenv
and
.BR ~/.rbenv ),
and, on macOS,
.B ~/.CFUserTextEncoding
and the keychains in
.BR ~/Library/Keychains ,
whose items the keychain itself guards.
So secrets, such as those in
.BR ~/.ssh ,
.BR ~/.aws ,
.BR ~/.netrc ,
or a shell's startup files, cannot be read, wherever in it they are.
On macOS, the directories above the fork, or
.IR dir ,
can be listed.
On Linux, every directory can be listed, though the files in it cannot be
read, and files made in the directories above the home directory after the
command starts cannot be read.
.PP
The network and the GPU are not confined.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B confine
sets
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SANDBOXED
to
.B 1
for the command, so that Claude Code trusts the directory without asking:
it would otherwise ask in every new directory, and
accepting would write to
.IR ~/.claude.json ,
which it may not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command's, as it becomes the command, or exits as it did.
If it cannot run the command,
.B confine
exits with 127 if the command was not found and 126 otherwise, as
.BR env (1)
does, and with 1 if the working directory cannot be forked, or if
.I dir
or a path given with
.B \-w
does not exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run Claude Code in a fork of the working directory:
.PP
.RS
.B confine \-w ~/.claude claude
.RE
.PP
Run the tests in a fork that is removed after them, so that they cannot
change the working directory:
.PP
.RS
.B confine \-\-rm make test
.RE
.PP
Run a shell that may write only to the working directory itself:
.PP
.RS
.B confine \-d .
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
On macOS, the command runs under Seatbelt, through
.BR sandbox\-exec (1),
which cannot run inside another sandbox, so a confined command cannot
confine another.
On Linux, it runs under Landlock, which needs Linux 5.19 or later; without
it,
.B confine
fails rather than run the command unconfined.
The command cannot run set-user-ID programs such as
.BR sudo (8)
on Linux.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-rm ,
the fork is left if
.B confine
is killed, by a signal other than those it ignores, before the command
exits.
.PP
The network is not confined: a command can send anything it can read to
anywhere, and can push to remotes whose credentials it can reach, such as
through the macOS keychain or an SSH agent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsdiff (1),
.BR fsfork (1),
.BR sandbox\-exec (1),
.BR landlock (7)
